air spots, and he had lost £600 (clue 6).", "proof": ["The castle was flattened between 22:30 and 00:30, while the gate was locked (clue 1). Only staff keys open the gate.", "Rowan's key opened the gate at 23:10 and 23:40 (clue 5).", "The size 11 zigzag prints lead to his hut, where the wet wellies were found (clues 1 and 3).", "So his claim that he hasn't been on the beach since eight is a lie (clues 5 and 6)."], "cleared": ["<b>Barnaby</b> was at sea from 21:00 to 05:30 (clue 2).", "<b>Esme</b> left at 21:55, while the castle was still standing at 22:30, and <b>Hilary</b> only came in at 05:50 – after the patrol found the castle flat at 00:30 (clues 1 and 5).", "<b>Colm</b> has no key and was home all night (clues 4 and 5). <b>Marisol</b> built the castle."]}}, {"slug": "the-last-canvas", "level": 2, "crime": "Art Forgery", "who": "forger", "title": "The Last Canvas", "teaser": "A ‘lost’ painting by a famous harbour artist sells for £40,000. The signature is perfect. The harbour in the picture is not.", "intro": "Tea-room owner <b>Marguerite Lowell</b> paid £40,000 for ‘Morning at Saltby’, a newly found painting by the harbour artist Augusta Fell. Now she fears it's a fake. Find all 6 clues, compare the statements and the evidence, and work out who did it.", "intro_text": "Tea-room owner Marguerite Lowell paid £40,000 for ‘Morning at Saltby’, a newly found painting by the harbour artist Augusta Fell. Now she fears it's a fake. Find all 6 clues, compare the statements and the evidence, and work out who did it.", "added": "2026-11-14", "daily": "2026-11-17", "public": "2026-11-17", "suspects": [{"name": "Amos Treloar", "role": "Harbour museum keeper"}, {"name": "Bettina Fell", "role": "The painter's great-niece"}, {"name": "Dr Leopold Grice", "role": "Art expert"}, {"name": "Juno Harker", "role": "Art student"}, {"name": "Marguerite Lowell", "role": "Buyer, tea-room owner"}, {"name": "Teddy Blackwood", "role": "Seller"}], "clues": [{"name": "Marguerite Lowell", "rol